{
  "trace_id": "hybrid::q04",
  "architecture": "hybrid",
  "question": "What is a silent failure, and why is it considered the most expensive category of failure?",
  "answer": "A silent failure is when the system returns an answer that looks confident, well\u2011formatted and correct, but in fact is wrong, incomplete, or based on faulty evidence\u2014and there is no indication in the output that anything went wrong. Because the response appears trustworthy, users accept it by default and only discover the error if they independently verify the claim, which rarely happens. This makes silent failures the most costly type of failure: they can go unnoticed and lead to decisions based on incorrect information, whereas visible errors (e.g., explicit refusals) are immediately apparent and can be addressed.\u202f[silent-failures-and-the-value-of-refusal::0]",
